WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.480
"Shang-Chi and the Ten Rings" brought dragons to&nbsp;
the Marvel Cinematic Universe, and left people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:13.440
wanting more. Whether they're androids or demons&nbsp;
from the moon, these dragons fascinate as friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:20.000
foes, or both. These are the biggest dragons from&nbsp;
Marvel Comics that we want to see in the MCU.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.720
Fin Fang Foom is one of the most enduring monsters&nbsp;
in the Marvel Universe. Created by Stan Lee and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:33.360
Jack Kirby in 1961's "Strange Tales" #89, he&nbsp;
was later incorporated into Marvel continuity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.440
and is a cross-media superstar, appearing&nbsp;
in comics, cartoons, and video games. Fin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.880
Fang Foom is so familiar to Marvel fans&nbsp;
that the glimpse of a massive, underwater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:51.600
dragon in the "Shang-Chi" trailers led to wild&nbsp;
speculation that we now know didn't pay off.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.200
Fin Fang Foom is an alien from the far-off world&nbsp;
of Kakaranathara and, when he's introduced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:03.280
lies dormant in an underground temple. A scholar&nbsp;
familiar with Fin Fang Foom's legend awakens him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:08.880
hoping to turn him against the invading forces&nbsp;
of Communist China — leading to a wall-whipping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.840
ship-smashing chase worthy of&nbsp;
"Looney Tunes" at its most chaotic.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.360
Fin Fang Foom's history in the Marvel Universe&nbsp;
almost defies description. He's fought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.040
Iron Man, Squirrel Girl, and — thanks to an&nbsp;
intercompany crossover — the Justice League&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.600
of America. He's converted to Buddhism, been&nbsp;
a chef, and lived on Monster Isle. He's even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.720
saved Christmas. All that's left for&nbsp;
this enduring comic book character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.800
is a long-overdue appearance in&nbsp;
the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:47.520
Ask any Marvel fan which classic Fantastic&nbsp;
Four villains they want to see in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.480
and odds are they'll name heavy hitters&nbsp;
like Doctor Doom and Galactus. But if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.560
the MCU wants to introduce an FF foe&nbsp;
never before seen onscreen, why not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:05.120
dig a little deeper into their rogues'&nbsp;
gallery? Why not introduce ... Dragon Man?

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:11.360
Joining Fin Fang Foom in the very exclusive&nbsp;
"dragon in little purple pants" club, Dragon Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:18.640
first appeared in 1965's "Fantastic Four" #35. An&nbsp;
android created by Professor Gregson Gilbert and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:24.800
brought to life by the evil alchemist Diablo,&nbsp;
Dragon Man was unleashed on the Fantastic Four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.800
during their visit to State University. He was&nbsp;
a creature of powerful strength and rudimentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:38.400
intelligence, until in true "King Kong" fashion,&nbsp;
a beautiful blonde -- Sue Storm -- soothed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.160
the savage beast. Brawling his way across&nbsp;
the Marvel Universe, he's fought Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.600
the Hulk, and even the pint-sized heroes of&nbsp;
Power Pack — but after Sue and Reed's daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:55.520
Valeria Richards upgraded his programming, he&nbsp;
became a valued member of the Future Foundation.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.920
"I was a force in the cosmos before the&nbsp;
worlds were made! I have brought whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:08.880
civilizations low! But do you really expect&nbsp;
to stand ... against the Dragon of the Moon?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:16.000
So boasted the eldritch horror imprisoned&nbsp;
on Titan in "The New Defenders" #138 (1984).

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.160
The Dragon of the Moon is a demon whose corrosive&nbsp;
influence can compel others to do evil deeds;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.360
to give an idea of its power, it once&nbsp;
claimed Thanos as one of its disciples.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.560
When the young psi-talent Heather Douglas trained&nbsp;
on Titan to unlock her mental abilities, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:38.560
Dragon attempted to take over her mind. Believing&nbsp;
that she had defeated it, Heather assumed the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:45.600
"Moondragon" as a symbol of pride. In actuality,&nbsp;
the Dragon of the Moon was slowly corrupting her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.480
Years later, it successfully turned&nbsp;
Moondragon against the Defenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:56.960
unleashing a threat that only ended after&nbsp;
several of her teammates sacrificed their lives.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.560
With its ability to corrupt&nbsp;
the seemingly incorruptible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.120
the Dragon of the Moon would be&nbsp;
a terrifying villain in the MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:11.680
One possible path toward the MCU is surprisingly&nbsp;
clear — in the comics, Heather Douglas is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:17.360
daughter of Drax the Destroyer and a member of&nbsp;
the Guardians of the Galaxy in her own right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:23.440
If Moondragon joins the onscreen Guardians, can&nbsp;
the Dragon of the Moon be lurking far behind?

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:29.520
Unlike the other Marvel dragons on this&nbsp;
list, Shou-Lao the Undying has technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:35.280
already appeared in the MCU ... if you count&nbsp;
a single pair of red eyes glowing in the dark.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:42.080
Introduced in "Marvel Premiere" #16, Shou-Lao is&nbsp;
an ancient dragon who dwells in the mystical city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:48.720
of K'un-L'un and is the power source behind the&nbsp;
Iron Fist mythos. A warrior must defeat Shou-Lao&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:55.040
the Undying, whose molten heart is hidden inside&nbsp;
a sacred cavern, if they are to absorb his power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:02.080
and become Iron Fist. Included in this heroic&nbsp;
lineage is the modern-day Iron Fist, Danny Rand.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:08.880
But when the 2017 "Iron Fist" television show on&nbsp;
Netflix welcomed Rand and K'un-L'un into the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.840
Shou-Lao was nowhere to be seen.&nbsp;
Though he was mentioned by name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:20.240
Shou-Lao never made a full appearance due&nbsp;
to one disappointing factor: the budget.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.600
As for Danny Rand himself, Finn&nbsp;
Jones, told a reporter in 2017:

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.800
"I'd love to have the budget for these shows&nbsp;
to have a full-on 'Game of Thrones' style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:36.960
dragon...But unfortunately, you know, we have&nbsp;
budget restraints. That's the nature of the show."

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:41.120
Netflix's "Iron Fist" ended&nbsp;
in 2018 after two seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:47.200
but the character's rich comic book history&nbsp;
is still worth exploring — and Shou-Lao&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.840
is still somewhere out there in the&nbsp;
darkness, waiting for his close-up.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:59.200
Superhero comics generally make bad playgrounds&nbsp;
for babies. If you're a baby in a superhero comic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:03.360
maybe you'll be lucky like Cable and&nbsp;
be sent into the future to become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:09.600
grizzled, cybernetic warrior. Or you could&nbsp;
be like Shogo Lee and turn into a dragon.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:15.280
Orphaned by a meteor crash and adopted&nbsp;
by X-Man (and former mallrat) Jubilee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:21.520
Shogo is a seemingly ordinary baby. Having the&nbsp;
X-Men for godparents automatically guaranteed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.960
Shogo a life of adventure, but fate had a&nbsp;
big surprise for Jubilee when she brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:32.080
Shogo through a magical gate into the realm&nbsp;
of Otherworld in "Excalibur" Vol. 4 #2.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.720
Fairy magic, combined with the unlimited power&nbsp;
of a child's imagination, spontaneously turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:45.760
Shogo into a giant, fire-breathing dragon. Now&nbsp;
the youngest member of Excalibur, Shogo acts as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:51.280
transportation and defense for his mother and&nbsp;
their teammates while in Otherworld.. Shogo the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:58.000
dragon would be an adorable addition to the MCU&nbsp;
— but watch out for those explosive tantrums.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:04.400
Before the dawn of the superhero,&nbsp;
there was ... Grogg! One of Fin Fang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:10.560
Foom's "Strange Tales" fraternity brothers,&nbsp;
Grogg first appears in "Strange Tales" #83&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.600
as a member of an ancient dragon race&nbsp;
held back by the Great Wall of China.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:21.600
In the spirit of creature features like "Gamera"&nbsp;
and "The Deadly Mantis," the prehistoric beast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:27.280
spends thousands of years sleeping beneath polar&nbsp;
ice until nuclear testing behind the Iron Curtain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:33.360
startles him. In his second appearance, Grogg&nbsp;
boards a Soviet rocket ship destined for Mars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.880
seemingly ending his&nbsp;
fire-breathing threat forever.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:40.960
The 2005 comic "Marvel Monsters:&nbsp;
Monsters on the Prowl" #1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:45.440
revealed that Grogg had been seized by the&nbsp;
Collector, becoming part of his macabre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.640
menagerie along with other "missing"&nbsp;
Silver Age comic book behemoths like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.240
Taboo and Grottu — though Grogg's&nbsp;
city-smashing days were far from over.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:00.080
Memorably played by Benicio del&nbsp;
Toro, the Collector is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.400
most slippery supporting characters&nbsp;
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:11.040
The true depths of his specimen collection are&nbsp;
unknown. Could Grogg be among them? If nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:17.440
else, it would be fun to see Grogg and Giant-Man&nbsp;
doing the MCU version of "Godzilla vs Kong."

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:25.040
Mr. Lao, a once-mortal man who gained eternal life&nbsp;
after a curse transformed him into a lung dragon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.520
did the only sensible thing with&nbsp;
his immortality: he got a job.

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:33.840
Acting as the advisor for the&nbsp;
Atlas Foundation, Mr. Lao utilized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:39.520
centuries of experience to become a master&nbsp;
strategist, with the added perk of ritually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:44.320
devouring every Atlas Foundation leader&nbsp;
after the appointment of their successor.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:48.800
Mr. Lao would eventually grant his&nbsp;
services to Agent of Atlas Jimmy Woo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:53.040
though the only agenda he&nbsp;
truly follows is his own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.560
Lao's Machiavellian intelligence sets him apart&nbsp;
from the more bestial members of this list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:04.720
and he would be an intriguing addition to&nbsp;
any upcoming MCU film or television series.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:10.400
Plus, after Randall Park's scene-stealing&nbsp;
turn as Agent Jimmy Woo in "Ant-Man and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:16.240
The Wasp" and "WandaVision," who wouldn't want&nbsp;
to see him trade quips with a colossal dragon?

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:18.000
"It's worth a try."

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:24.320
Flying past the finish line is the X-Men's&nbsp;
diminutive dragon friend, Lockheed. Debuting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:31.520
in "Uncanny X-Men" #166 by Chris Claremont and&nbsp;
Paul Smith, Lockheed is a member of the alien race&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:36.800
known as the Flock. With the X-Men trapped&nbsp;
in space during the "Brood Saga," Lockheed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:42.320
rescued Kitty Pryde by torching the brood aliens&nbsp;
hunting her. Accompanying the X-Men back to Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:48.640
Lockheed helped Kitty fight off more alien&nbsp;
hatchlings — and a beautiful friendship was born.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:53.280
Given his small size, Lockheed is&nbsp;
often regarded as a pet, but he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:59.040
is highly intelligent and communicates&nbsp;
in several languages, including English.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:04.000
Apart from the X-Men, he has worked with SWORD,&nbsp;
guarded the Time Gem with the Pet Avengers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.120
and squared off against fellow dragon Fin&nbsp;
Fang Foom. Lockheed can usually be found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:12.480
perched on Kitty's shoulder, whether&nbsp;
they're dimension-hopping with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:17.520
Excalibur in the "Cross-Time Caper" or&nbsp;
sailing the high-seas in "Marauders."

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:23.040
Lockheed had a cameo in "The New Mutants,"&nbsp;
the final Fox "X-Men" film, alongside Magik,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:28.480
Kitty's comic book best friend. But after the&nbsp;
failure of "The New Mutants" at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:32.080
it would be a shame to consign&nbsp;
Lockheed to cinematic limbo.

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:35.440
Marvel Studios' plans for&nbsp;
the X-Men remain unknown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:40.800
but if Kitty Pryde appears in the MCU&nbsp;
— as either the wide-eyed junior X-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:45.840
or the swashbuckling Captain Kate —&nbsp;
Lockheed is sure to be flying by her side.

